# Raw wire schema drift

Owner's correction (issue #382): the schema is minted from the **raw wire body**. The IR is
internal and is not diffed. Mapping raw request to IR is #392 and is out of scope.

## Two checks, only two

| Check | Question | Gates? | Outcome |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Structure | Did the shape of the request change? | yes | `EXACT` / `DEGRADED` / `BREAKING` |
| Content | Was a system prompt or tool definition updated? | never | a report |

## What already exists and is correct

`baseline_capture.py` already captures A/B/A through the real launch path into a fresh bare
`source_home` per probe. `ProbeEvidence` already carries `raw_request_base64`, `raw_nodes`
(`request_inventory::observe_request_json`) and `inventory` (`build_request_inventory`).
Every one of those is raw wire. None of it changes.

## What is wrong today

1. `AbaAnalysis.observed_schema` is a flat pointer list carrying **concrete array indices**.
   A 13th tool changes the pointer set, so adding a tool reads as structural drift. It is content.
2. `compare_baseline_bundles` returns `BREAKING` when `static_fingerprint` changes. That
   fingerprint is over **values**. An edited system prompt is therefore breaking today. It must
   never gate.
3. `DriftOutcome` carries `COMPATIBLE` and `INSUFFICIENT`.
4. Structure and content are decided in one function over one entangled pointer set.

## Target

### `request_schema.py`, new, top-level leaf beside `request_inventory.py`

Pure over raw JSON. Knows nothing about baselines, probes or the IR.

`mint_request_schema(bodies: Sequence[object]) -> RequestSchema`

A recursive observed JSON Schema. The union of every observation at one position.

- object: `properties` is the union of keys, `required` is the keys present in **every**
  observation of that node, `additionalProperties` is false.
- array: `items` is the union over **every element of every observation**. Array indices
  collapse. This is the load-bearing decision: it makes "a 13th tool of a known shape" `EXACT`
  and leaves "a tool gained a field" as the only structural signal.
- scalar: `type` is the sorted set of observed JSON type names.

`compare_request_schema(reference, candidate) -> StructureVerdict`

Walk both trees together.

| Observation | Verdict |
| --- | --- |
| identical | `EXACT` |
| candidate has a property the reference lacks | `DEGRADED` |
| candidate type set is a strict superset | `DEGRADED` |
| a reference-required property is absent from candidate `properties` | `BREAKING` |
| type sets are disjoint (retyped) | `BREAKING` |
| body does not parse | `BREAKING` |

Precedence `BREAKING` > `DEGRADED` > `EXACT`. The verdict carries the pointer and reason for
every finding, so nothing is decided by an unexplained enum.

**Open decision.** A reference-required property that is present but merely optional in the
candidate is a weakened guarantee, neither "removed" nor "new". Proposed: `BREAKING`, because
a consumer that assumed presence can now fail. Alternative: `DEGRADED`.

### `baseline_evidence.py`

`DriftOutcome` becomes exactly `EXACT` / `DEGRADED` / `BREAKING`. `COMPATIBLE` and
`INSUFFICIENT` are deleted.

Bootstrap stops being a sentinel outcome. `reference_outcome: DriftOutcome | None`, with
`reference_bundle_id is None` if and only if `reference_outcome is None`. Illegal states
become unrepresentable instead of being spelled `INSUFFICIENT`.

`repeat_a_outcome: DriftOutcome` becomes `runtime_generated_pointers: tuple[JsonPointer, ...]`.
The A1 versus A2 leg never answered "is this drift". Its real job is naming the one or two
runtime-generated values, which is the data the content check needs. An enum that lied becomes
the data every caller wanted.

`compare_content(reference, candidate) -> ContentReport` over stable pointers only, excluding
`runtime_generated_pointers`. Reports changed, added and removed value pointers with digests,
and the value itself for string leaves. Returns no outcome and contributes to no outcome.

### `baseline_store.py`

`promotes_baseline` becomes `reference_outcome is None or reference_outcome is EXACT`.
`artifact_schema_version` 2 becomes 3. No migration; the store already hard-rejects a foreign
version with "regenerate the baseline".

## Known limitation

Content pointers keep concrete array indices, so reordering tools reports as changes. Content
never gates, so this is report noise rather than a correctness defect. Semantic identity for
tools (keying by name) is provider-specific and is not worth the coupling yet.

## Reuse Map

Audited before any implementation. A new helper for a capability named here is a defect.

| Need | Existing symbol | Disposition |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Name a JSON value's type | `json_tags::json_kind` | **Reuse.** `request_inventory` already casts its result to `JsonKind`. Minting must not re-derive type names. |
| Strict JSON decode of a raw body | `request_inventory::_decode_json` | **Promote to public and reuse.** It rejects duplicate keys and NaN/Infinity. `baseline_evidence::classify_aba`, `codex/request_parser`, and `adapters/anthropic` each call bare `json.loads` on raw bodies today. That is existing duplication and it gets collapsed onto this one door. |
| Stable digest of a structure | `canonicalization::canonical_digest` / `canonical_json` | **Reuse.** |
| Enumerate every raw node with pointer, kind, digest | `request_inventory::observe_request_json` -> `RequestJsonNode` | **Reuse for content.** Not used for structure: it carries concrete array indices, which is the defect being fixed. |
| String leaf values with provenance | `request_inventory::build_request_inventory` -> `RequestStringLeaf` | **Reuse for content.** |
| Pointer ancestor / subtree relation | `baseline_evidence::_covers` | **Reuse.** Escaping-aware; `/tools/1` is correctly not an ancestor of `/tools/10`. |
| RFC 6901 pointer build and parse | `request_inventory::_pointer`, `_escape_pointer_token`, `_pointer_tokens` | **Reuse.** Promote only if the schema walker needs them. |
| JSON Schema over a request body | none | **Genuinely new.** Confirmed absent from all of `api/src`. The only Pydantic JSON Schema hooks are in `space/models.py` for a custom scalar. |

Adjacent but distinct, deliberately not merged: `drift_capture::detect_unknown_shapes` with
`adapters/anthropic::KNOWN_REQUEST_EXTRA_KEYS` and `codex/request_parser::KNOWN_INPUT_ITEM_KEYS`
is a **hand-maintained allowlist** of fields TM knows about. The observed schema is **minted from
capture**. They answer different questions and neither replaces the other.

## Also decided by the audit

`ProbeEvidence.normalized_request` is produced by `session/wire_normalization::normalize_request`,
which is IR-derived. It is evidence rather than gate input, but under the owner's ruling it has no
business in a raw-wire bundle. Proposed: delete the field. It is the last IR residue in the artifact.

## Decisions closed 2026-08-19

1. **BREAKING is wire-only and TM-blind.** The wire lost a field it always sent, or retyped one.
   Decided from the two schemas alone. TM-dependency cannot be an input, because the raw-to-IR
   mapping is #392 and is out of scope.
2. **A field that was always present becoming sometimes-absent is BREAKING.** A guarantee observed
   three for three is gone. Does not promote.
3. **The outcome decides baseline promotion only.** It does not touch harness certification.
   Owner's ruling: certification *should* gate on this eventually, but certification asks whether
   TM can still handle a harness version, which is a raw-to-IR mapping question. The certification
   gate therefore hangs off #392 and consumes #382's schema. This issue produces the schema and the
   outcome; it wires them into nothing else.
4. **Array `items` branches per discriminator tag, it does not collapse into one union.**
   Established by probing real captures, not by argument. `codex_response_create.json` shows
   `input[]` is a discriminated union on `type`: `message`, `reasoning`, `function_call`,
   `function_call_output`, `custom_tool_call_output`, plus an untagged plain message whose only
   keys are `content` and `role`.

   A flat union over those five gives `required` = the empty intersection and `properties` = a soup
   of every variant's keys. Two consequences, both fatal: a `function_call` that stops sending
   `name` is not BREAKING because `name` was never required in the soup, and a brand new variant
   (`type: "web_search_call"`) built from already-seen keys is EXACT. A new variant is the single
   highest-value drift signal TM exists to catch.

   Minted shape: group an array's object elements by their tag token, preferring `type`, falling
   back to `role`, falling back to one untagged bucket. Each group mints its own subschema and the
   array becomes `items: {oneOf: [...]}`. Then a 13th tool of a known type merges into its existing
   branch and stays `EXACT`, a new variant adds a branch and reads `DEGRADED`, and a variant losing
   a required key reads `BREAKING`. Reuse `json_tags::literal_tag` and `json_tags::json_kind` for
   the tag token; the repo already models this domain in
   `codex/request_parser::KNOWN_INPUT_ITEM_KEYS`, which is literally tag to key set.

## Proof, run before any production code

Prototype minted a tag-branched schema from the real capture
`api/tests/fixtures/codex_response_create.json`. Branches discovered, recursively and without any
hand-written vocabulary:

```
/input/*             type=function_call          required=[arguments, call_id, id, name, type]
/input/*             type=function_call_output   required=[call_id, id, output, type]
/input/*             type=message                required=[content, id, role, status, type]
/input/*             type=reasoning              required=[encrypted_content, id, summary, type]
/input/*             role=user                   required=[content, role]
/input/*/content/*   type=input_text             required=[text, type]
/input/*/content/*   type=input_image            required=[detail, image_url, type]
/input/*/content/*   type=output_text            required=[annotations, text, type]
/input/*/summary/*   type=summary_text           required=[text, type]
/tools/*             type=function               required=[description, name, parameters, strict, type]
/tools/*             type=file_search            required=[type, vector_store_ids]
```

Three drift probes against that reference, all correct:

| Mutation | Verdict |
| --- | --- |
| add a 13th tool of a known type | `EXACT` |
| harness adds `input` item `type: web_search_call` | `DEGRADED`, new variant at `/input/*` |
| `function_call` stops sending `name` | `BREAKING`, `/input/* type=function_call` lost required `name` |

The untagged bucket is real and is exercised: the first `input` element carries only `content` and
`role`, so it lands on `role=user` rather than on any `type` branch.

Guard: if tag branching yields more than twelve branches at one array, the tag is not behaving as a
discriminator and the array falls back to a single unioned `items`.
